Durarara!! AU
Chapter 44 – Part 1
The phone rang while Curtis was still finding a shirt that did not smell like last night.
Mika. No greeting. Full volume.
“I’m coming to collect! Where do you live!”
He pinched the bridge of his nose. The gauze reminded him it was there.
“Mika? Where’s Seiji?”
A scoff. City noise behind her. A door that did not belong to his building.
“With the girl-shaped problem, where else. He won’t leave the room. Namie-san is doing that face she does when she wants to lock windows. I told her I was getting air. I am getting you. Address. Now. You promised the truth and you still owe me three.”
Curtis sat on the edge of the bed.
Celty’s dent was still in the blanket. The oni was in the closet. The door was unlocked because he had told a dullahan it would be.
“I sent the address last night.”
“I closed the chat because Seiji was looking. Send it again. If you make me wander Ikebukuro with a reconstruction hangover I am adding a fourth.”
He sent it again.
Curtis: unit number is in the text. do not climb a window. if namie calls you come back. truth first. food. then we argue about counting.
Mika: already walking. you better not be in a mask.
Curtis looked at the closet, then at the couch, then at the kitchen Namie had already inventoried.
“Yeah,” he said into the phone. “No mask. Where’s Seiji, Mika. For real.”
“I told you. He picked her. I picked air. You picked a scary rider instead of your own face.” A brighter, meaner sweetness. “Today you pick me at your door. Ten minutes. If Namie-san follows me I am not turning around.”
The line died.
Curtis stared at the dent in the bed.
“Seiji’s in the room,” he told the empty flat. “Namie’s doing the window face. Mika’s collecting. Celty’s on a job.” He dragged the shirt on, hid the foam case deeper, and kicked last night’s tie under the bed. “Cheap seats.”
Justice would have been on the dresser, howling.
Curtis put coffee on instead, unlocked nothing extra, and waited for a knock that was not a helmet.
Curtis flicked his fingers.
White tendrils slid out and went to work while he still had minutes. Mop across the kitchen tile. Wipe down the table Namie had already read as a lie. Blanket pulled straight until Celty’s dent was just a couch again. The tie vanished deeper under the bed. Coffee cups became two instead of a night’s worth.
He stretched until the shoulder complained, black compression shirt pulling tight across the map he was not showing, joggers hanging easy, braids falling forward.
“Okay. Good enough.”
The knock came like it had been practicing.
Curtis opened the door.
Mika Harima stood in the hall in clothes that were not a hospital’s, hair doing its best impression of a girl who had walked out of a locked building on purpose. She took him in at once — height, gauze, heterochromia, the fact that the foreigner who had promised a face was wearing the kind of shirt that made the hallway feel smaller.
“You look like you fought a building and then did yoga.”
“Good morning to you too.”
She pushed past him without waiting to be invited, then stopped in the middle of the furnished lie and turned a slow circle.
“This is nice. Too nice. You said college kid.” Her eyes snagged on the bandage, then on the two mugs, then on the closet he had shut too carefully. “No mask. Good. If that demon answers the next knock I am adding a fourth and a slap.”
Curtis shut the door.
“Seiji still in the room?”
“Yes. Namie-san texted twice. I muted her.” Mika pointed at the couch like she had bought it. “Sit. You promised the truth about why a scary rider showed up instead of you. Then we argue about three. Then you feed me. In that order.”
Curtis stayed standing one second longer than she wanted.
The smoke had done its job.
The room looked innocent.
The girl collecting kisses did not know a dullahan had been on that couch before sunrise.
“Alright,” he said. “Truth first. Sit down, Mika.”
Mika did not sit.
She pointed at his hairline like the gauze had just confessed.
“That head bandage. Where did you get that head wound. You weren’t there last night.”
Curtis let the door finish being shut.
“I was there.”
“No. He was there. The tall one. The mask. The voice. People streamed it. You texted me from a couch.” Her mouth tightened. “Don’t do the kind voice. I already had a company do the kind voice while they put my face on a table.”
He walked past her, poured the second coffee, and set it where she could ignore it.
“The scary rider was me.”
Mika stared.
The furnished room got very small.
“That’s not funny.”
“I’m not joking. I told you I couldn’t walk in as me. The city was a riot. Yagiri had guns in a kitchen. If Curtis Nightstar showed up in a school shirt, they film a first-year and they keep shooting. If the other face shows up, they film a problem and I get you out of the white room.” He tapped the gauze once, gentle. “Helmet cracked. That’s this. Stream owned a quarter of it. I got you down the stairs. Then I texted like a coward because the mask was gone and I still didn’t want the name on your phone yet.”
Mika’s laugh came out wrong.
“You promised you. I got a demon.”
“You got out.” Curtis’s voice stayed even. “You wanted the truth about why. That’s why. I was there. I was the one you called scary. I’m the one standing here without it.”
She looked at his eyes like she was trying to fit red lenses over them.
“Take off the bandage.”
“No.”
“Then how do I know you’re not lying again.”
“Because if I were lying I’d have a better story than I put a monster on my face and still failed the part you could see.” He sat. Finally. “You can be angry. You should be. You don’t get to unpack the rest of the night like it’s a closet. That wound is from the helmet. The rest is mine.”
Mika sat down hard on the couch that was not as empty as it looked.
“Three kisses just became a trial,” she said, but the joke had less air in it. “Say it again. Slow.”
Curtis met her eyes.
“I was the Tech Rider last night. That’s where the bandage came from. That’s why I wasn’t standing there as Curtis. You’re free. I still owe you the face I promised.”
Mika wrapped both hands around the mug and did not drink.
“Namie-san is going to lose her mind.”
“She already has enough on her plate. This stays in this room unless you decide it doesn’t.”
Mika’s gaze flicked to the closet, then back to the gauze.
“Show me the mask.”
Curtis exhaled through his teeth.
“After coffee,” he said. “And after you tell me Seiji didn’t follow you.”
They drank the coffee like it could make the sentence smaller.
It didn’t.
Curtis stood, went into the bedroom, and came back with the foam case. He set the oni on the table between the mugs.
Horns. White plates. Black jaw. Red lenses dead in the daylight. F3RC stamped in the brow. Caution marks. Fangs that had never been a toy.
“My other one you saw last night is destroyed. This is one from L.A.”
Mika leaned in despite herself. The mask looked like the stream. Close enough to make her shoulders climb. Different enough that she could tell it had not taken the bullet.
Curtis looked down.
“I’m sorry, Mika.”
She was out of the chair before the apology finished.
The hug hit him mid-chest, sudden and fierce, the way she did everything. For a second he went still — bandage, coffee, demon on the table — then his arms came up around her because leaving her hanging after a white room would have been another broken promise.
“You idiot,” she said into the compression shirt. “You sent a monster and then you sat here looking like a person. I wanted the person.”
“I know.”
“Don’t I know me.” Her fists bunched in the black fabric. “You got me out. I still get to be mad. Those two things can sit together.”
“They can.”
She didn’t look at the oni while she held him. That felt like mercy.
Curtis kept the hug clean. No counting. No door fee. Just a girl who had been inventory and a boy who had worn a face so the city would shoot at the wrong thing.
On the table, the L.A. mask stared at the ceiling with its lights off.
Mika’s voice came muffled.
“If Namie-san texts again I’m still muted. Seiji can keep the room. You can keep being sorry for ten more seconds. Then you feed me. Then we talk about whether three is still three.”
Curtis let out a breath that shook once and then behaved.
“Ten seconds,” he said. “Then food. Mask stays on the table. It doesn’t go back on my face while you’re here.”
Mika nodded against him and did not let go early.
They sat.
The oni stayed on the table with its eyes off. Mika kept stealing looks at it, then at him, like she was still trying to make one face fit the other.
Curtis turned the mug in his hands.
“So. What now, Mika? You got Seiji. You’re no longer being hunted. No Black Rider. Now what?”
Her head snapped up.
“Does this mean we can’t see each other?”
“What? No.” He set the mug down so the answer would not hide in porcelain. “I mean what do you plan to do from here. I still want to see you and hang out.”
Mika blinked, then scowled like he had accused her of being easy to discard.
“You say now what like the job closed. I just hugged you over a demon mask. Don’t file me under finished.”
“I’m not. The job was get you out. That’s done. The rest is you deciding if you go back to school, stay with Namie and Seiji, climb out more windows, or eat someone else’s cooking twice a week.” A small tilt of his mouth. “I’m offering the cooking. And the hanging out. Not as a rider. As me.”
She pulled her knees up on his couch, suddenly younger than the reconstruction and older than the joke about kisses.
“Seiji picked the room. I picked air. I don’t know what I am if I’m not being looked for.” The honesty landed and she hated it, so she kicked it. “I can still collect three. That’s a plan.”
“That’s a bit.” Curtis’s voice stayed kind. “You can have a plan that isn’t a bit. You’re allowed to want Seiji and still come here. Those two things can sit together too.”
Mika stared at him.
“You’re weirdly decent for a guy who owns that.” She jerked her chin at the horns. “Fine. I don’t know now what. I know I don’t want this to be the last time I sit on this couch. If you disappear behind another mask I will find this building again.”
“I won’t disappear on purpose.” He met her eyes. “Hang out. Food. Texts that aren’t apologies. When you figure the rest out, you tell me. I’ll still be around.”
Mika’s mouth twitched.
“Still three,” she said, because she needed a joke that belonged to her.
“Still not a door fee,” he said, because he needed the line to stay clean.
The hunted part of her life had ended in a white room and a cracked helmet.
What sat on the couch now was just a girl asking if she still had a place, and a boy who was not closing the file.
Mika did not argue the number.
She climbed into his lap, knees on either side of the compression shirt, and locked her mouth to his like the apology had been a countdown.
Curtis made a sound that was surprise first. His hands found her waist out of reflex, not invitation, holding so she would not tip them both off the couch. The kiss was not shy. Mika collected the way she did everything — all in, no invoice.
One.
He kissed her back for that one. Then he turned his face enough to break it without shoving her.
“Mika—”
“That was one.” Her cheeks were high. Her eyes were bright and a little scared under it. “You owe two. Don’t make it a speech.”
“I’m not making it a speech.” His voice had gone rough. He kept his hands where they were, steady, not traveling. “I’m making sure you want a kiss and not a hole Seiji left. Those are different.”
Mika’s mouth twisted.
“I know what I want. I climbed out of a building. I muted Namie-san. I sat through a demon on your table. This is me picking air.” She leaned in again, softer. “Two can wait if you’re going to look at me like I’m broken.”
Curtis exhaled against her hairline.
“You’re not broken. You’re fast.” A beat. “One happened. Food still happens. Two and three don’t get decided because you’re on my lap and I said I was sorry.”
She stayed where she was, forehead tipped to his, breathing like she had run.
“Fine. One in the bank. You still have to hang out with me. That part isn’t optional.”
“That part I already said yes to.”
The oni watched from the table with its lights off.
Curtis did not chase the rest of the count.
Mika did not climb down yet.
Outside, Namie’s muted thread filled with a sister’s name. Seiji kept the room. Celty kept the morning’s courier list.
On the couch, one kiss sat between them, collected and unfinished, and for the moment that was as far as either of them let the door open.
Curtis’s hands tightened at her waist.
He pulled her in closer, the decision sitting in his shoulders this time, and their mouths found each other again.
Two.
Mika made a small sound into it and held his face with both hands, thumbs at his jaw, careful of the gauze even while she was not careful about anything else. The kiss was slower than the first one. Less collection. More stay. Her knees framed him. The compression shirt had nowhere to hide how close she had gotten.
He let it happen.
The apartment held its breath around a girl who had been hunted and a boy who had promised a face. Coffee went cold. The oni on the table kept its dead red eyes. Somewhere Namie’s thread stayed muted.
When they parted it was only far enough to share air.
Mika’s palms stayed on his cheeks.
“Two,” she whispered, like she had to name it or it would turn into something she could not keep.
Curtis’s forehead rested against hers. His hands did not leave her waist.
“Two,” he agreed. His voice had dropped. “You still here because you want to be. Not because I owe you a mask.”
“I’m here because I want to be.” She searched his mismatched eyes like she was daring them to become lenses. “Three can wait. Don’t you dare file this under sorry.”
“I won’t.”
They stayed like that — her holding his face, him holding her there — until the knock of the city felt far enough away to ignore.
He did not put the mask on.
She did not climb down.
Two kisses sat in the room with them, and the third stayed uncollected on purpose.
Mika hopped off him like the couch had gotten hot.
She landed on her feet, ears pink, hands doing nothing useful at her sides.
“I um. Seiji’s never kissed me like that. Ever.”
Curtis stayed on the couch. He did not reach. He did not smile like he had won a room from another boy.
“That’s not a contest,” he said, gentle. “That’s just different people.”
Mika’s blush went darker.
“I know. I didn’t mean— he looks at me. He chose the room. He doesn’t—” She gestured at her own mouth, then at him, then gave up. “You kiss like you mean the person is there. He kisses like I’m already the ending.”
Curtis rubbed the back of his neck, careful of the gauze.
“You can still want the room, Mika. I’m not trying to steal a house. Two kisses don’t evict him.”
“I know that too.” She dropped onto the far cushion this time, not his lap, and hid half her face behind the mug. “Don’t say anything nice. I’ll combust. Feed me before I start counting three out loud.”
He got up because food was safer than the look she was trying not to give him.
“Food,” he said. “No speeches. Seiji keeps the room. You keep coming here if you want. Those two things can still sit together.”
Mika peeked over the mug, pink and stubborn.
“They better. I muted Namie-san for this.”
Curtis went to the kitchen with his mouth still remembering her hands on his face, and he did not turn that into a plan.
Two was two.
Seiji had never kissed her like that.
That was her sentence to carry, not his to cash.
Mika set the mug down like she had decided to be brave about the wrong thing.
“Does it bother you I have a boyfriend.”
Curtis’s hands stilled on the fridge door.
“That’s complex for me to explain.”
He saw Justice for a second, not as a ghost with a joke, as the boy who would have already been on the couch with an answer that sounded like a song. Justice collected rooms. Justice could hold two names in one night and call it honest because nobody was being lied to about the math. Justice would have grinned and said then we don’t pretend it’s simple and kept cooking.
Curtis was not him.
Curtis had always been the kinder one. The one who stopped the count. The one who asked if a kiss was a hole someone else had left. The one who said two things could sit together and then actually tried to make the sitting kind.
He shut the fridge without taking anything out.
“It bothers me if I’m a secret you use to hurt him. It bothers me if you have to mute the whole building to sit here.” He leaned on the counter, eyes on her, not on the oni. “It doesn’t bother me that you love Seiji. That’s your house. I kissed you knowing the house exists. That’s on me too.”
Mika’s mouth opened. Closed.
“Justice would have made that sound cooler,” Curtis added, quieter. “He was better at not making people feel like a problem. I’m just… trying not to be cruel about it. You’re not a bit to me. He’s not a rival. I’m not him.”
“The brother.”
“Yeah. He would have already been laughing. I made coffee and asked you not to treat three like rent.” A small, tired shrug. “Kinder. Slower. Worse at the line. That’s the complex part. I can want you in this room and still not want to burn the one you already have.”
Mika looked at her hands.
“So it does bother you.”
“It bothers me when people get hurt by the math. It doesn’t make me want you to leave.” He opened the fridge again, for real this time. “Food. Then you decide if hanging out still works while he has the room. I won’t decide it for you.”
Mika nodded once, blush still there, joke finally late to the party.
“You’re worse at this than a demon mask,” she muttered.
“I know,” Curtis said, and meant the kindness, not the horns.
Curtis went into the kitchen and took a sharp knife off the magnet strip.
He bounced it off the counter. The blade turned once, clean, and he caught it mid-air like the handle had always known his palm. He set the board. Vegetables lined up. The knife went to work in short, even strokes.
Mika watched from the doorway.
He flicked the second knife up off the wood, caught that one too, swapped without looking, and kept chopping. No flourish. No grin. His face stayed the same quiet it had been when he said the math was complex — concentration, not performance.
It was simply how he prepped food.
“You cook like that in L.A.?” Mika asked.
“I cook like that since I had to make dinner mean we ate.” He didn’t glance up. Onion. Pepper. The catch again, unnecessary to anyone who had grown up with drawers that stayed shut. “Hands remember. Brain’s on the pan.”
The knives stopped being a trick and became a rhythm. Board. Blade. Catch. Board.
Mika leaned on the frame and did not say show-off, because his eyes had never asked her to clap.
“Seiji burns toast,” she said, instead, like that was safer than I just kissed you twice.
Curtis huffed once, almost a laugh, and slid the vegetables into the pan.
“Then you’re getting food that isn’t toast. Sit. If I look at you I’ll miss the catch and we’ll both be mad at the counter.”
She sat.
He cooked like the forest and the bad years had taught his hands a language that did not need an audience.
The oni stayed on the table with its lights off.
The kitchen smelled like someone intended to keep a promise that was not a mask.
Curtis brought the pan to the table like it was the only honest thing in the room.
The dish looked like it had no business coming out of a first-year’s kitchen. Color. Steam. A plate set for two instead of an apology.
He went back for the bottles.
One was wine with a label that did not belong next to instant coffee. The other was sparkling juice, cold, unopened, the same courtesy he had given anyone who had never asked for alcohol.
He set both down between the oni and the food.
“It’s ready.”
Mika stared at the plate, then at the two bottles, then at him.
“You just did circus knives and now you’re running a restaurant.”
“I’m running lunch.” He turned the juice toward her without pushing it. “Wine’s there if you want it. Juice is there if you don’t. I don’t pour for people who didn’t ask.”
She took the juice. The blush from the couch had not fully left.
Curtis sat, poured himself a small measure of the wine, and did not make a toast. The mask watched the meal with dead lenses. Outside, Namie was still muted. Seiji still had the room.
Mika picked up her fork.
“If this is good I’m coming back,” she said.
“That’s the idea.”
They ate.
For a few minutes the truth, the bandage, the two kisses, and the boyfriend question all sat in the other chair and let the food be the thing that happened next.
Curtis ate like a gentleman.
Knife and fork set right. Pace even. Napkin where it belonged. No elbows claiming the table, no talking with his mouth full, no performance of hunger after a night that had earned it. He looked like the furnished apartment had finally met the person it was dressed for.
Mika watched him over her juice, surprised into stillness.
“Where did you learn all this.”
Curtis swallowed, set the fork down, and did not reach for the easy joke.
“Justice and I learned together. We had to. Forest doesn’t come with manners. Cabins we stole from had books. Picture pages. We copied what looked like people who weren’t starving.” A small, private smile. “Then we started cooking together. He was chaos at a cutting board. I was the one who wanted the food to come out the same twice. We taught each other the rest. How to sit. How not to make a table feel like a raid.”
Mika’s eyes flicked to the empty third chair that wasn’t there.
“The group too?”
“Yeah. I did most of the cooking for him and our friends. Somebody had to or we’d have lived on whatever came in a bag. Justice could make a room laugh. I could make it eat.” He picked the fork back up. “Still works that way. Hands remember the knives. Back remembers the table.”
Mika looked at her plate like it had become a history lesson.
“So the demon mask and the fancy wine and the please-and-thank-you all came out of the same two kids.”
“Same two kids.” Curtis’s voice stayed even. “One of them’s gone. The other one still sets two bottles down so nobody feels pushed.”
She drank the juice.
“Seiji would have already spilled something,” she muttered, not unkind.
Curtis did not take the opening.
He ate like Justice was still across from him, correcting a wrist, stealing a bite, calling him kind like it was an insult and a compliment at once.
Mika watched, and for the first time since the white room the scary rider and the boy with manners occupied the same face without her needing the horns to make sense of it.
Curtis finished, stacked the plates, and took them to the sink.
Mika waited until the water was running.
“How did you run down the building side and pull out a white scythe.”
His hands stopped. Soap on his wrists. Shoulders set like the question had weight.
Mika didn’t let the silence win.
“It is the same way you made the pink rose the night at the hotel?”
Curtis stared at the water a second longer than dishes needed.
“I’m afraid I can’t tell you how it works or where it comes from.”
“Why?”
“There’s a blowback if I try. It’s disturbing for others to see.”
He shut the tap. Turned enough that she could see he meant it — not a tease, not a secret for later.
“I start the sentence and my body collects the fee. Blood. Passing out. It looks like I’m dying on the floor for a name I am not allowed to say. I am not putting that on you after a white room and coffee.”
Mika’s brow knit.
“So the rose, the scythe, the smoke that cleaned this place before I knocked—”
“Same family. Different tricks. I can use them. I cannot give you the instruction manual.” He dried his hands, slower than the knives had been. “You already got the truth I promised. The rider was me. The helmet is why my head is wrapped. The rest stays in the drawer because the rest bites.”
She looked at the oni on the table, then at him.
“That’s a terrible answer.”
“It’s the only safe one.” A faint, tired almost-smile. “If I ever try and I start coughing red, you call nobody except me telling you to look away. That’s the deal. Not a demonstration.”
Mika hugged her own elbows.
“Fine. I hate it. I’m not asking you to bleed for a how-to.” She tipped her chin at the sink. “You still wash dishes like a gentleman. That’s allowed to make sense.”
Curtis turned the water back on.
The rose stayed a hotel memory.
The scythe stayed a building wall.
The name that would have opened his throat stayed where it belonged — unsaid, unpaid, and not on Mika Harima’s afternoon.
Curtis stood at the sink with the towel in his hands and did not move.
Should I just show her.
The apartment looked too clean for what the sentence cost. Two mugs. An oni with its lights off. A girl who had already watched a building try to keep her.
He looked around once — couch, door, phone — then at Mika.
“If I show you. You can’t freak out and you can’t call an ambulance. Okay?”
Mika went still.
“That is the opposite of okay.”
“I’m not asking you to like it. I’m asking you not to turn it into a hospital. It looks worse than it is. It passes. If you call someone, I get questions I still can’t answer and you get a night that isn’t lunch.”
Her mouth flattened.
“You just said it was disturbing. Now you want to do it on purpose.”
“I want you to stop thinking I’m holding a toy back for fun.” He set the towel down. “I still won’t say the name. I can show you what happens when I try. That’s the fee. You stay in that chair. You don’t touch me until I say so. You don’t call Namie. You don’t call anyone.”
Mika’s fingers dug into her sleeves.
“And if I say no?”
“Then we don’t. Food stays food. The rose stays a rose. I wash the rest of the dishes.”
She stared at him for a long second, Seiji’s room and two kisses and a white scythe all stacked behind her eyes.
“If you die I’m going to be so mad at you.”
“I’m not going to die.”
“That’s what people say right before they do something stupid.” Mika swallowed. “Okay. I won’t freak out. I won’t call an ambulance. If you make me a liar I am adding three back with interest.”
Curtis nodded once.
He sat on the floor with his back to the cabinets, where a fall would only be tile.
“Last chance to keep it a terrible answer,” he said.
Mika didn’t move from the chair.
“Show me.”